---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import GuideTable from '../../components/GuideTable.astro';
import { bySlug, destMonthUrl, MONTHS } from '../../lib/data';

const ALL = MONTHS.map((_, i) => i);
const rows = [
  { slug: 'cabo-san-lucas', label: 'Cabo San Lucas' },
  { slug: 'puerto-vallarta', label: 'Puerto Vallarta' },
  { slug: 'mexico-city', label: 'Mexico City' },
  { slug: 'san-miguel-de-allende', label: 'San Miguel de Allende' },
  { slug: 'oaxaca', label: 'Oaxaca' },
  { slug: 'cancun', label: 'Cancún & Riviera Maya' },
  { slug: 'belize', label: 'Belize' },
  { slug: 'roatan', label: 'Roatán' },
  { slug: 'antigua-guatemala', label: 'Antigua, Guatemala' },
  { slug: 'guanacaste', label: 'Guanacaste' },
  { slug: 'puerto-viejo', label: 'Puerto Viejo (Caribbean)' },
  { slug: 'panama', label: 'Panama City' },
];
// The driest destination in each rainy-season month.
const driest = [5, 6, 7, 8, 9].map((m) => {
  const d = rows.map((r) => bySlug.get(r.slug)!).sort((a, b) => a.months[m].rain - b.months[m].rain)[0];
  return { m, d };
});
---

<Guide slug="rainy-season-mexico-central-america" photo="antigua-guatemala">
  <p>
    Across most of Mexico and Central America, the <strong>rainy season runs from May or June to October</strong>, and the dry
    season from November or December to April. But "rainy" rarely means all-day rain: the typical pattern is a sunny morning
    and a heavy afternoon or evening downpour. The bigger risk is the <strong>Atlantic and Pacific hurricane season</strong>
    (June–November), which peaks from August to October.
  </p>

  <h2>Rainfall by month</h2>
  <GuideTable caption="Average monthly rainfall in Mexico and Central America" months={ALL} show="rain" rows={rows} />
  <p class="small muted">Average monthly rainfall from national climate normals.</p>

  <h2>Weather rating by month</h2>
  <GuideTable caption="Weather rating in Mexico and Central America by month" months={ALL} rows={rows} />

  <h2>Where it's driest in the rainy season</h2>
  <ul>
    {driest.map(({ m, d }) => <li><b>{MONTHS[m]}:</b> <a href={destMonthUrl(d, m)}>{d.name}</a> ({d.months[m].rain} mm)</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>Region by region</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Baja California (Cabo San Lucas):</b> desert-dry nearly all year; the little rain comes with late-summer storms in August–October, when hurricanes are also possible.</li>
    <li><b>Pacific Mexico (Puerto Vallarta):</b> a sharp wet season from June to October, very hot and humid, with the heaviest rain in July–September.</li>
    <li><b>Central highlands (Mexico City, San Miguel, Oaxaca):</b> afternoon thunderstorms from June to September, but mornings are usually sunny and temperatures mild at altitude.</li>
    <li><b>Yucatán and the Caribbean (Cancún, Belize, Roatán):</b> showers from June, with the most rain and hurricane risk in September–October. Belize and Roatán's wettest months run into November–January, when cold fronts arrive.</li>
    <li><b>Guatemala and Costa Rica's Pacific side:</b> classic green season from May to November, wettest in September–October, with a drier spell (the <i>canícula</i> or <i>veranillo</i>) in July–August.</li>
    <li><b>Caribbean Costa Rica and Panama:</b> wet most of the year, but often drier in September–October, when the Pacific side is at its wettest.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Is the rainy season worth it?</h2>
  <p>
    Often, yes. Prices drop, the landscapes are green and the crowds are gone, and on most days you'll still have a sunny
    morning. Plan outdoor activities early, keep afternoons flexible and buy travel insurance that covers hurricanes. Avoid
    September–October on the Caribbean coast and in the Yucatán if you can, and check our guides to{' '}
    <a href="/guides/hurricane-season/">hurricane season</a>, <a href="/guides/sargassum-season/">sargassum seaweed</a> and the{' '}
    <a href="/guides/costa-rica-green-season/">Costa Rica green season</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
